DEEP[1]=('Chapter 12, Bhakti Yoga, the yoga of devotion, is the shortest chapter of the Gītā at 20 verses. Many families learn it by heart; it is often the first chapter children are taught to chant.',
 'Arjuna has just seen the cosmic form and heard that devotion alone reveals it (11.54). Now he asks whether loving the personal Lord is better than meditating on the formless.',
 'Ask which path suits you, not which path is best in the abstract.')
DEEP[2]=('',
 '<i>Āveśya</i>, “making enter”: the mind is made to enter the Lord, as water enters a sponge. Madhva says devotion to the personal Lord, with knowledge, is clearly superior. The Bhāgavata Purāṇa builds much of its theology on this answer.',
 'Let the mind sink into what you love most. That absorption is itself yoga.')
DEEP[3]=('',
 'The eight descriptions echo the Upaniṣads, especially the Bṛhadāraṇyaka description of the Akṣara (3.8.8) and the Māṇḍūkya (7). Śaṅkara treats this path as the highest, the path of the renunciant knower. Rāmānuja reads <i>akṣara</i> here as the individual self in its pure state, so the comparison is between meditating on the self and on the Lord.',
 'The formless is real but hard to hold in mind. Respect those who walk that path.')
DEEP[4]=('',
 '<i>Sarva-bhūta-hite ratāḥ</i> repeats 5.25 exactly. Commentators note that even the most inward path must include love of all beings. Śaṅkara says those on the formless path reach the Lord because they realise themselves as one with him. Rāmānuja says they reach the self, and through it, the Lord. The verse prevents any rivalry between paths: both arrive.',
 'Whatever your path, care for all beings is the sign you are really on it.')
DEEP[5]=('',
 'Śaṅkara explains that the difficulty comes from identifying with the body: one who still feels “I am this body” cannot easily hold the bodiless as one’s goal. Rāmānuja says meditation on the self alone is harder than meditation on the Lord, who helps the meditator. Ramakrishna often said that the path of devotion is easier for most people in this age.',
 'Choose a path that fits who you are now. You can grow from there.')
DEEP[6]=('',
 'Commentators see three yogas joined here: <i>sannyasya</i>, offering action (karma yoga); <i>dhyāyantaḥ</i>, meditating (dhyāna yoga); <i>upāsate</i>, worshipping (bhakti yoga). The verse recalls 3.30 and 9.27. Rāmānuja says this is the complete practice of devotion. The sentence completes in 12.7 with the Lord’s promise.',
 'Offer your work, keep the highest as your aim, and remember with love. Those three together are the path.')
DEEP[7]=('',
 '<i>Samuddhartā</i>, “the one who lifts out”, is a vivid image: the Lord reaching into the ocean and pulling out the drowning. In the Purāṇic story of Gajendra, the king of elephants, caught by a crocodile, called on Viṣṇu, who came at once and freed him. Commentators often cite it. <i>Na cirāt</i>, “before long”: help is not distant.',
 'You do not have to swim the whole ocean alone. Call, and trust that help comes.')
DEEP[8]=('',
 '<i>Manas</i>, mind, is the feeling and wishing part; <i>buddhi</i>, intellect, the deciding part. Both are to be given to the Lord. Commentators call 12.8–11 the ladder of devotion, with four rungs. Śaṅkara says it offers paths to seekers of different capacities. Rāmānuja says each step leads to the next. The ladder is famous because it leaves no one out.',
 'Begin with the highest you can. If it is too hard, step down one rung. There is always a rung for you.')
DEEP[9]=('',
 '<i>Abhyāsa-yoga</i>, practice, is defined by Śaṅkara as repeatedly drawing the mind back from all objects and fixing it on one. Practical forms include japa, reciting the Lord’s names; regular meditation; and daily worship. Patañjali (Yoga Sūtra 1.14) says practice becomes firm when done for a long time, without break and with devotion.',
 'Repetition builds habit. A few minutes of daily practice, kept up, does more than occasional bursts.')
DEEP[10]=('',
 '<i>Mat-karma</i>, work for the Lord, is read by Śaṅkara as worship, pilgrimage, temple service and the like. Rāmānuja includes all actions done as service. Modern teachers widen it further to service of people, <i>sevā</i>. Many Sikh and Hindu traditions centre on sevā: cleaning, cooking for community kitchens, caring for the sick. The verse promises that this too reaches perfection.',
 'If sitting still is hard, serve. Washing, cooking, helping: service is also a full path.')
DEEP[11]=('',
 'The last rung: do whatever you do, and give up its fruits. Commentators note the ladder ends where the Gītā began (2.47). Śaṅkara says this is for one who cannot even do work specifically for the Lord. Rāmānuja says it means karma yoga that leads to self-knowledge and then to devotion. The beauty of the verse is its accessibility: everyone works, so everyone can practise this.',
 'Even if nothing else is possible, you can do your work and let the outcome go.')
DEEP[12]=('',
 'This verse has puzzled commentators because 12.11 placed letting go of fruit at the bottom of the ladder. Śaṅkara explains that practice without understanding is mechanical; understanding without meditation is dry; meditation with attachment is incomplete; letting go brings peace directly. Rāmānuja says letting go of fruit is praised here to encourage those who can only do that. <i>Anantaram</i>, “immediately”: peace is not a future reward.',
 'Letting go is not the easy option. It is the doorway to peace, and it can open now.')
DEEP[13]=('',
 'Verses 12.13–20 list some 35 qualities of the devotee dear to the Lord. Patañjali (Yoga Sūtra 1.33) recommends <i>maitrī</i>, friendliness toward the happy, and <i>karuṇā</i>, compassion toward the suffering, to calm the mind. The Buddhist <i>brahma-vihāras</i> begin with the same two. Commentators note that the devotee is defined first by how they treat others.',
 'Hate no one; be friendly and kind. That is where devotion shows first.')
DEEP[14]=('',
 'The refrain <i>sa me priyaḥ</i>, “he is dear to me”, echoes through 12.14–20. Commentators find it moving that the Lord speaks of what he loves in a person. <i>Santuṣṭa</i>, content, is listed in Patañjali as <i>santoṣa</i>, one of the five <i>niyamas</i> (Yoga Sūtra 2.32), and is said to bring unsurpassed happiness (2.42). Rāmānuja says such devotees are dear because they love the Lord for his own sake.',
 'Contentment, steadiness and offering make a life beautiful. Practise one of them today.')
DEEP[15]=('',
 '<i>Amarṣa</i>, impatience or envy at others’ success, is a subtle fault. Commentators note the two-way peace: the devotee does not trouble the world, and the world cannot trouble him. Śaṅkara says no creature fears such a person. Gandhi used this verse to describe the ideal satyāgrahī: one who harms no one and is not frightened by anyone.',
 'Be a calm presence. Don’t disturb others, and don’t let them disturb your peace.')
DEEP[16]=('',
 '<i>Dakṣa</i>, skilful or capable, is often overlooked. Commentators note that the Gītā’s devotee is competent, not dreamy. Śaṅkara says <i>dakṣa</i> means able to act quickly and rightly when needed. <i>Śuci</i>, pure, covers outer cleanliness and inner purity. <i>Sarvārambha-parityāgī</i>, giving up undertakings, is read as giving up ventures for selfish gain, not all work.',
 'Be competent and free of expectation. Together they are rare and beautiful.')
DEEP[17]=('',
 'Four reactions released: elation at the pleasant, hatred of the unpleasant, grief at loss, craving for what is absent. Commentators read <i>śubhāśubha-parityāgī</i> as giving up attachment to the fruits of good and bad deeds. Madhva adds that the devotee leaves the accounting to the Lord. The verse is close to 2.57 and 5.20, now spoken of a devotee rather than a sage.',
 'Let go of keeping score. Love remains when the scoreboard is put away.')
DEEP[18]=('',
 'Commentators link this verse with 6.7–9 and 14.24–25, where the same pairs appear. Śaṅkara says equanimity toward friend and foe comes from seeing the one Self in both. Rāmānuja says the devotee sees all as related to the Lord. For Arjuna, about to face cousins and teachers, the line is pointed: fight, but without hatred.',
 'Treat friend and foe with the same fairness. It is strength, not weakness.')
DEEP[19]=('',
 '<i>Maunī</i>, silent, does not mean never speaking but speaking little and wisely. Commentators note that indifference to praise and blame is especially hard for public figures. <i>Aniketa</i> is read by Śaṅkara literally, as the wandering monk with no home; by others as not attached to one’s home. <i>Yena kenacit</i>, “with anything at all”: contentment that does not choose.',
 'Speak less, want less, and notice how contentment grows in the quiet.')
DEEP[20]=('',
 '<i>Dharmyāmṛta</i>, “nectar of dharma”, names the teaching of 12.13–19. Commentators note the shift from <i>priya</i>, dear, to <i>atīva priya</i>, exceedingly dear. Śaṅkara says those still striving to reach these qualities are dearest. Rāmānuja says the Lord’s love increases for those who follow with faith. The chapter that began with a question about paths ends with the Lord’s love. The colophon names it Bhakti Yoga.',
 'You don’t have to be perfect to be loved. Sincere effort with faith is enough.')
